# Reduction (Lift) of an $H$-torsor to $H'$

## Setup

Let $X$ be a base scheme (the definitions adapt verbatim to topological spaces, manifolds, or any site with the appropriate topology — fppf, fpqc, étale, etc.). Let $H$ be a group scheme over $X$, and let $P \to X$ be an **$H$-torsor** (a principal $H$-bundle): a scheme $P$ with a right $H$-action that is locally trivial in the chosen topology, so that locally on $X$ one has $P \simeq H$ with the right-regular action.

Let $\varphi: H' \to H$ be a homomorphism of group schemes over $X$ (most commonly a **closed immersion**, i.e. $H'$ is a subgroup scheme of $H$; we then write $H' \hookrightarrow H$ and suppress $\varphi$ from the notation).

## Definition

A **reduction** (also called a **lift**) of the $H$-torsor $P$ to $H'$ (along $\varphi$) is a pair
$$
(P',\, \alpha)
$$
consisting of:

1. an **$H'$-torsor** $P' \to X$, and
2. an **isomorphism of $H$-torsors**
$$
\alpha:\; P' \times^{H'} H \;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\; P,
$$

where $P' \times^{H'} H$ denotes the **contracted product** (extension of structure group from $H'$ to $H$):
$$
P' \times^{H'} H \;=\; (P' \times H)\,/\,H',
$$
with $H'$ acting diagonally — on the right on $P'$ (the torsor action) and on the left on $H$ (via $\varphi$ and left multiplication). The quotient carries a residual right $H$-action (on the second factor), making it an $H$-torsor over $X$.

In words: $P$ is obtained from $P'$ by extending the structure group along $\varphi: H' \to H$, and $\alpha$ identifies the result with the given $H$-torsor. One says that $P$ **admits a reduction to $H'$** when such a pair $(P',\alpha)$ exists.

## Equivalent characterizations

**(A) Via a section of $P/H'$.** When $\varphi$ is a closed immersion $H' \hookrightarrow H$, the quotient $P/H'$ (right cosets) exists as an $X$-scheme with a natural projection $\pi: P/H' \to X$. Then:

> A reduction of $P$ to $H'$ is equivalent to a **section** $\sigma: X \to P/H'$ of $\pi$.

*Construction in both directions:*
- Given $(P', \alpha)$: the composition $P' \to P'\times^{H'}H \xrightarrow{\alpha} P \to P/H'$ factors through $P'/H' \simeq X$ (since $P'/H'$ is a point), yielding a section $\sigma: X \to P/H'$.
- Given $\sigma$: set $P' := P \times_{P/H'} X$ (fiber product along $\sigma$). The right $H'$-action on $P$ (via the inclusion $H' \hookrightarrow H$) descends to $P'$, making $P'$ an $H'$-torsor, and the natural map $P' \times^{H'} H \to P$ is an isomorphism.

**(B) Via local trivializations with transition functions in $H'$.** Choose a cover $\{U_i\}$ of $X$ trivializing $P$, with transition functions $g_{ij}: U_{ij} \to H$. A reduction to $H'$ is equivalent to refining the cover so that all transition functions factor through $H'$:
$$
g_{ij} = \varphi \circ g'_{ij}, \qquad g'_{ij}: U_{ij} \to H',
$$
and the $g'_{ij}$ satisfy the cocycle condition in $H'$. The $H'$-torsor $P'$ is then glued from the trivial pieces $U_i \times H'$ using the $g'_{ij}$.

**(C) Via a sub-torsor.** When $H' \hookrightarrow H$ is a closed subgroup, a reduction is equivalent to a **closed $H'$-stable subscheme** $P' \subseteq P$ such that the action map $P' \times H' \to P'$ makes $P'$ an $H'$-torsor and the induced map $P' \times^{H'} H \to P$ is an isomorphism. (This is just the image of $P' \hookrightarrow P$ under the inclusion, compatible with characterization (A).)

## Remarks

- **Terminology.** "Reduction of structure group" and "lift of the structure group" are used interchangeably; "reduction" emphasizes that $H'$ is smaller (one reduces the amount of structure), while "lift" emphasizes that one lifts the transition data from $H$ to $H'$.
- **Functoriality.** Reductions along $\varphi$ are classified (up to isomorphism) by the fiber of the map $H^1(X, H') \to H^1(X, H)$ in non-abelian cohomology, sending $[P'] \mapsto [P'\times^{H'} H]$. Thus $P$ admits a reduction to $H'$ iff its class $[P] \in H^1(X,H)$ lies in the image of this map.
- **Obstruction.** When $\varphi$ is a closed immersion with quotient $H/H'$, the obstruction to reducing $P$ is the vanishing of the class of the associated $H/H'$-bundle $P/H' \to X$ admitting a section; equivalently, the class in $H^1(X, H/H')$ (when this makes sense, e.g. $H'$ normal) must be trivial.

